8.1. Using a .NET Class Library
using System;
namespace LibraryDemo
{
class Program
{
static void Main(string[] args)
{
double number = 25;
double squareRoot = Math.Sqrt(number);
double power = Math.Pow(5, 2);
Console.WriteLine("Number: " + number);
Console.WriteLine("Square Root: " + squareRoot);
Console.WriteLine("5 raised to power 2: " + power);
Console.ReadLine();
}
}
}
Output
Number: 25
Square Root: 5
5 raised to power 2: 25

8.2. Creating a Class Library
Open Visual Studio 2019.
1: Start a new project
1. Open Visual Studio 2019.
2. Click Create a new project.
3. Select Class Library (.NET Framework) if you are using the .NET Framework.
4. Click Next.
2. Configure the Project
Enter the following details:
Field Value
Project Name MyCalculatorLibrary
Location Choose any folder
Solution Name ClassLibraryDemo
Framework .NET Framework 4.7.2 or the version available
Click Create.
Visual Studio creates a project containing a file called:
Class1.cs
3. Create a Calculator Class
Right-click Class1.cs in Solution Explorer and select:
Rename → Calculator.cs
Replace the existing code with:
using System;
namespace MyCalculatorLibrary
{
public class Calculator
{
public int Add(int a, int b)
{
return a + b;
}
public int Subtract(int a, int b)
{
return a - b;
}
public int Multiply(int a, int b)
{

return a * b;
}
public double Divide(int a, int b)
{
if (b == 0)
throw new DivideByZeroException();
return (double)a / b;
}
}
}
4. Build the Class Library
From the Visual Studio menu, select:
Build → Build Solution
If there are no errors, Visual Studio creates a DLL file.
For example:
MyCalculatorLibrary.dll
The DLL is normally located under a folder such as:
bin\Debug\
A Class Library is compiled into a DLL (Dynamic Link Library) that can be reused by
other applications.
5. Create a Console Application
Now create another project to use the Class Library.
Right-click the Solution in Solution Explorer. .(In MyCalculatorLibrary project only)
Add → New Project
Choose:
Console App (.NET Framework)
Name it:
CalculatorApp
Click Create.

Your solution now contains two projects:
ClassLibraryDemo
│
├── MyCalculatorLibrary
│ └── Calculator.cs
│
└── CalculatorApp
└── Program.cs
6. Add a Reference to the Class Library
The Console Application needs to know about the Class Library.
Step 1
Right-click:
CalculatorApp
in Solution Explorer.
Select:
Add → Reference...
Step 2
Select:
Projects → Solution
You should see:
MyCalculatorLibrary
Select the checkbox beside it.
Click:
OK
Now the Console Application can use the classes contained in the Class Library.
7. Use the Class Library
Open:
Program.cs
using System;

using MyCalculatorLibrary;
namespace CalculatorApp
{
class Program
{
static void Main(string[] args)
{
Calculator calc = new Calculator();
Console.WriteLine("Addition: " + calc.Add(10, 5));
Console.WriteLine("Subtraction: " + calc.Subtract(10, 5));
Console.WriteLine("Multiplication: " + calc.Multiply(10, 5));
Console.WriteLine("Division: " + calc.Divide(10, 5));
Console.ReadLine();
}
}
}
Note: In Solution Explorer right click on CalculatorApp project and select Set as Startup
Project.
8. Run the Program
The output will be:
Addition: 15
Subtraction: 5
Multiplication: 50
Division: 2
